=== REASONING ===

Why this design preserves ADR-015, P1–P10, and the non-AI baseline:

1. ADR-015 §I3 (baseline intact). The deterministic
   VehicleConfigSection (model, trim, current exterior color,
   software version) and the manual per-vehicle theme/appearance
   settings remain the canonical, always-rendered surface for
   vehicle appearance. The AI section is an OPT-IN propose-only
   draft layered alongside; the AI section is rendered AFTER
   the VehicleConfigSection (delay 0.27 vs 0.26) so that even
   in on-mode the baseline appears first. The AI surface never
   replaces the photo gallery, the per-vehicle Color setting,
   or any typed mutation handler.

2. ADR-015 §I5 (hidden UI in off mode). The React component
   is wrapped with `withAiFeature('vehicle-paint-preview', …)`,
   which returns null when ai_mode='off' OR the per-feature
   toggle is false. The off-mode test
   `TestVehiclePaintPreviewAIOffHidesPreviewTool` exercises both
   halves of that gate plus a positive control to defeat the
   trivial-true failure mode.

3. ADR-015 §I6 (404 routes in off mode). The backend route
   POST /api/v1/ai/vehicles/{vehicleID}/paint-preview/draft is
   wrapped with `ai.GuardedHandler('vehicle-paint-preview', …)`
   in internal/api/ai_routes.go. The Go-side
   `TestVehiclePaintPreviewAIOffHidesPreviewTool` proves the
   route returns 404 when ai_mode='off' AND when the per-feature
   toggle is false.

4. ADR-015 §I7 (per-feature opt-in). The registry entry uses
   `DefaultOn: false` so a fresh install with ai_mode='cloud'
   still shows nothing until the user opts in via the AI Settings
   page. The mode→off transition clears the per-feature toggle
   per the existing F0 behaviour.

5. ADR-015 §I10 (type system). The strategy and tool wire only
   typed DTOs (PaintPreviewProposalDraft / paintPreviewEvidence /
   paintPreviewProposal). No raw SQL, no string templating into
   write paths. The tool's evidence envelope INTENTIONALLY OMITS
   the vehicle's display_name and VIN even though the read
   already happened — defence-in-depth at the data plane against
   a renegade LLM that ignores the system-prompt redaction
   directive (mirrors the share_card_image.go pattern).

6. P1 (provider port) — the strategy is registered through
   provider.For + dispatch.New, never importing the provider
   adapters directly.

7. P4 (strategy/dispatcher loop) — Run() is the only execution
   path; the handler hands off after seeding the system + user
   messages and a deny-all confirm hook (the slice never opens
   the F4 confirm-pause flow because there are no mutating tools).

8. P5 (decorator chain) — provider.For applies the locked
   redaction → rate-limit → cost-cap → audit → trace stack on
   every call from the dispatcher loop.

9. P6 (frontend useAiEnabled + backend GuardedHandler) — both
   layers reference the same registry feature ID
   `vehicle-paint-preview`.

10. P8 (eval goldens) — three deterministic goldens
    (midnight_blue, arctic_silver, refusal_other_vehicle) +
    canned mock-provider replies. The refusal golden enforces the
    same-entity scoping rule that defends against cross-vehicle
    prompt drafting.

11. P9 (registry coverage) — `Routes` populated with explicit
    Backend + Frontend + UITestIDs entries; JobNames + PushKinds
    set to `[]string{}` (explicit-empty per the slice prompt).

12. P10 (baseline coexistence) — the surface composes through
    the parent VehicleDetailPage; selection between baseline +
    AI is purely the visibility gate from withAiFeature, not a
    branch inside business logic.

13. P11/P12 (wired-or-absent + no-placeholder-buttons) — the
    component imports useAiStream, references the registered
    backend endpoint, has zero placeholder strings, and the
    on-mode wiring test
    `TestVehiclePaintPreviewAIOnWiredCallsRoute` proves the
    button enqueues exactly one POST against
    /api/v1/ai/vehicles/7/paint-preview/draft and consumes
    the SSE stream.

14. HX (Helix UX) — the surface renders through AIFeatureCard;
    the per-feature verb "Preview paint color" is passed via
    `buttonLabel`; the AIBadge label is "Helix"; the on-mode
    test locates the CTA via UNANCHORED regex
    `/Preview paint color/i` (the accessible name is
    "Ask Helix · Preview paint color"); user-visible i18n keys
    say "Helix", not "AI" (`vehicles.aiPaintPreview.title`,
    `…description`, `…badge`, `…noVehicleHint`,
    `…streamingLabel`).

Architectural note: this slice does NOT bind the vehicleID into
the tool's typed envelope as a separate scope-context field.
The defence against cross-vehicle prompt drafting relies on (a)
the system-prompt refusal directive, (b) the
refusal_other_vehicle golden enforcing that pattern, and (c) the
tool's evidence envelope omitting display_name/VIN entirely so
even a successful cross-vehicle call would only receive the
vehicle's redacted shape (model/trim/color) — never an
identifier. This matches the established 0060 + auto-trip-naming
pattern; introducing a different scope-binding shape ONLY in
this slice would make it a snowflake against the rest of the
URL-bound feature corpus.

